{
  "slug": "binary-tree-level-order-traversal",
  "title": "Level Order Listing",
  "topic": "trees",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "trees / BFS levels",
  "statement": "# Level Order Listing\n\nThe tree is given as a level-order array `root`: the children of the entry at index `i` live at indices `2i+1` (left) and `2i+2` (right). An entry holding `null` means that child is absent and is skipped while traversing.\n\nReturn an array with one entry per depth level, where each entry lists that level's values from left to right. `null` holes are skipped and never appear in the output. An empty tree yields `[]`.\n\nConstraints: `0 <= root.length <= 30` and `-100 <= root[i] <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "root = [3,9,20,null,null,15,7]",
      "output": "[[3],[9,20],[15,7]]",
      "explanation": "Depth 0 holds 3; depth 1 holds 9 and 20 left to right; depth 2 holds 15 and 7 under node 20."
    },
    {
      "input": "root = [1]",
      "output": "[[1]]",
      "explanation": "A single node forms one level containing just itself."
    },
    {
      "input": "root = [1,null,2]",
      "output": "[[1],[2]]",
      "explanation": "The null left hole is skipped, so 2 (the right child) stands alone on depth 1."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function levelOrder(root: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Handle levels one at a time, not nodes one at a time.",
    "Only enqueue children that are actually present.",
    "Collect each level's values before moving deeper."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Breadth-first sweep: process one depth level at a time, queuing each node's present children for the next round.",
    "why_optimal": "Every present node is enqueued and dequeued once, giving linear time with a queue bounded by the widest level. On 29 PAF-verified cases the judge measured the brute force at 0.61 ms and the optimal at 0.26 ms (about 2.3x faster).",
    "pitfalls": "Skip null holes without emitting placeholders, and return an empty array for an empty tree."
  },
  "reading_links": [
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breadth-first_search"
  ],
